  "market_context": "Backtested over one week during high-volume London to New York session overlap. Market exhibited clear market structure (higher highs/lows in uptrends, lower highs/lows in downtrends) with frequent impulse moves followed by retracements.",
  "key_observation": "Most of the 100 trades retraced to the 0.5–0.618 Fibonacci 'gold zone', where entries with 1:1.5–1:1.6 R:R produced substantially more wins than losses, yielding profitable daily and weekly results.",
  "analysis_steps": [
    "Step 1: Identify market structure using higher highs and higher lows (or lower highs/lows) to determine trend direction during London–NY session.",
    "Step 2: Locate Break of Structure (BOS) points that define the start of an impulse move.",
    "Step 3: Draw Fibonacci retracement from the swing high to low (or vice versa) of the impulse move.",
    "Step 4: Focus entry zones on the 0.5 to 0.618 retracement area (Gold Zone), where price most frequently pulled back before continuing the trend.",
    "Step 5: Place stop loss below 0.618 (for longs) or above 0.618 (for shorts), and set take-profit at previous swing or Fib extension levels to achieve 1:1.5–1:1.6 R:R.",
    "Step 6: Execute trades only during high-volume London to NY session to ensure reliable structure and follow-through."
  ],
  "outcome": "Overall profitable across 100 trades; wins substantially outnumbered losses, resulting in profitable days and weeks.",
  "lessons": "Consistent profitability comes from trading high-probability confluences—market structure + BOS + Fibonacci gold zone—during high-volume sessions, with disciplined 1:1.5+ R:R sizing.",
